 @danielrouw2593  no, let me tell you why. In video games frames are calculated based on random input from player, unlike video editing where what will be done is already laid out by video editor guy before rendering. 

Now, remember RTX 4060 low vram problem where lower vram reduces performance? It's because modern gpus use GDDR6 which is kinda slow. (compared to HBM). Now the next frame player will create randomly (let's say your character shoots randomly in GTA 5) will be calculated in iGPU then will be transfered to your GDDR6 controller in discrete GPU and then processor will look if the frame is in a acceptable standard and will apply its own protocol to said frame. 

All this adds so much latency it will also block next frames from being displayed so you will get from 150 fps to 20 fps. Or you may choose to not display this iGPU rendered frame in the first place, which is what modern computers do by using discrete GPUs when available. :)

