 @Noliving  the reason this is relevant is because it displays the intention of the manufacturer.  A manufacturer debuting a card at $330 and then two later dropping the price to $200 is not the same as a manufacturer debuting a card at $200 from launch.  It displays the intention of the manufacturer to make more profit over winning over the market with a “hero” style card.  Where as Intel is dead set on being that “hero” style card being one with their brand on it.

Example here, nvidia trying to justify $399 for their 4060, which was already on 4050 silicon, and then suddenly dropping the price because AMD was beating them in value.  This shows nvidia’s intention of swinging their d1ck thinking that their customers will buy nvidia no matter what, and being sorely mistaken when the sales figures came out a month later.

Intention matters.  When nvidia launched the 4060, all the reviewers said “it’s not worth $400,” and none of the big reviewers went back and re-reviewed the product when nvidia lowered the price.  This is why intention matters, because the first impression lost them millions in revenue and 4060’s are still sitting on store shelves even at their lower price now.

